    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21596",
      "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/040ac76a28687db1c99d3379242b726a85aa4baf269a7c6d35d9662af1d3bd78.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/64e74d25a35e457d5aed550ed1e0d72898427afd01c1793190db15f23ee35afb.svg)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/fb3f325a2410ef34a81cceb2c6f8945482f6ec27c998340da6eeaae1321ff0a1.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/a28ae499a5f671f3c6194a664612cb30389a72e572b227f28536f204eaea5166.svg)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/eae4e99ae271a66074f38227b6dced4a8687ccbad2c8406ebe43e327c471db7a.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/fce7e9d630be70315e6d83cc9f61b4e245e8886f6d94a006b5656b71c0b32e23.svg)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/954dc69f82256912521e2a58ee38976b4ccc77a1e495a004a8fffab521d961ef.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/fb648bfcf3a4a86e8a616a99cfce38ced483b114e06954b543ec2c7af11a5575.svg)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14121715/assets/91c074766b92790a0e78b768b0290d7723617d6ed30e51e842501cad63e58a44.png)",
